ernment\nL53: \nL54: Britain is a monarchy.\nL55: \nL56: 3\nL57: : a government having a hereditary chief of state with life tenure and powers varying from nominal to absolute\nL58: \nL59: The country's hereditary monarchy survived for centuries.\nL60: ## Examples of monarchy in a Sentence\nL61: \nL62: the French monarchy of the 18th century\nL63: \nL64: Recent Examples on the Web\nL65: \nL66: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【20†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL67: Employees allege the company is run like a monarchy, with those at the top ruling with impunity. — Alessandra Schade, Vanity Fair, 28 Oct. 2026 Britain and the monarchy have struggled with these issues before. — ABC News, 28 Oct. 2026 In recent years, the British monarchy has adopted a more conciliatory tone when addressing the horrors of slavery. — Rupert Neate, CNN Money, 27 Oct.\nL68: 2026 Advertisement Several branches of the Japanese imperial family renounced their royal status in 1947 to cut down on costs the monarchy incurred after the war. — Chad De Guzman, Time, 26 Oct. 2026 【21†See All Example Sentences for monarchy】\nL69: ## Word History\nL70: \nL71: First Known Use\nL72: \nL73: 15th century, in the meaning defined at 【22†sense 2】\nL74: \nL75: Time Traveler\nL76: \nL77: The first known use of monarchy was in the 15th century\nL78: \nL79: 【23†See more words from the same century】\nL80: \nL81: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL82: \nL83: 【24†monarchomachic】\nL84: \nL85: monarchy\nL86: \nL87: 【25†monarda】\nL88: \nL89: 【26†See all Nearby Words】\nL90: \nL91: ## Articles Related to monarchy\nL92: \nL93: 【27†A Royal Vocabulary Quiz Get your dukes in a row.】\nL94: ## Cite this Entry\nL95: \nL96: Style\nL97: \nL98: [Select]\nL99: \nL100: “Monarchy.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/monarchy. Access