†merriamwebster.threadless.com】\nL28: \nL29: 【14†Dictionary】\nL30: \nL31:   * # republic\nL32: ## 【15†noun】\nL33: \nL34: re·​pub·​lic 【16†ri-ˈpə-blik 】\nL35: \nL36: plural republics\nL37: \nL38: 【17†Synonyms of republic】\nL39: \nL40: Simple Definition A Simple Definition is available from our Learner's Dictionary to help you understand the meaning faster.\nL41: \nL42: 1\nL43: \nL44: a\nL45: \nL46: : a form of government in which the power belongs to a body of citizens entitled to vote and is exercised by the leaders and representatives elected by those citizens to govern according to law compare 【18†democracy sense 1b】\nL47: \nL48: b\nL49: often Republic plural Republics : a country, state, or territory having a republican (see 【19†republican entry 2 sense 2a】) government\nL50: \nL51: I pledge allegiance to the flag of the United States of America and to the Republic for which it stands …—Francis Bellamy\nL52: \nL53: The United States, which the eighteenth-century American elite sought to refashion as a new Roman Republic …—Michael Lind\nL54: \nL55: also : a usually specified government of such a country, state, or territory\nL56: \nL57: the French Fourth Republic\nL58: \nL59: c\nL60: : a country, state, or territory that is headed by someone other than a hereditary monarch but whose citizens do not hold real power\nL61: \nL62: the former Soviet republics\nL63: \nL64: Note: In modern times, this sense is used in the names of various countries whose forms of government vary greatly and include 【20†dictatorships】 and 【21†totalitarian】 regimes.\nL65: \nL66: see also 【22†banana republic】\nL67: \nL68: 2\nL69: \nL70: a\nL71: figurative : a community of beings thought to resemble a political republic especially by exhibiting a general equality among members\nL72: \nL73: … a curious republic of industrious hornets …—Michel-Guillaume-Saint-Jean de Crèvecoeur\nL74: \nL75: b\nL76: \nL77: : a group of people freely engaged in a specified activity\nL78: \nL79: a republic of scholars\nL80: [Zora Neale] Hurston makes explicit two contradictory and submerged elements of that tradition: First, and most visibly, she restores funkiness and folk roots to black women's discourse; second, and no less important, she dares to articulate black women's craving for independent recognition in the republic of letters. [=the people who read and write literature]—Elizabeth Fox-Genovese\nL81: \nL82: 【23†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】\nL83: ## Did you know?\nL84: \nL85: Is the United States a democracy or a republic?\nL86: The United States is both a democracy and a republic, as both terms refer to forms of government in which supreme power resides in the citizens. The word __republic_ refers specifically to a government in which those citizens elect representatives who govern according to the law.\nL87: The word democracy can refer to this same kind of representational government, also called a 【24†representative democracy】, or it can refer instead to what is also called a 【25†direct democracy】, in which the citizens themselves participate in the act of governing directly by voting for all laws, policies, decisions, etc.\nL88: ## Synonyms of republic\nL89: \nL90: Relevance\nL91: \nL92:   * 【18†democracy】\nL93: \nL94: 【17†See All Synonyms & Antonyms in Thesaurus】\nL95: ## Examples of republic in a Sentence\nL96: \nL97: When asked by a passerby what sort of government the constitutional convention had formulated for the new nation, Benjamin Franklin memorably replied, “A republic, if you can keep it.”\nL98: \nL99: Recent Examples on the Web\nL100: \nL101: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【26†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL102: Some portray figures integral to the early history of the Haitian republic. — Elisa Turner, Miami Herald, 29 Oct. 2026 Since Charles’ accession in 2022, several Commonwealth realms have renewed discussions about becoming republics. — Brooke N. Newman, The Conversation, 29 Oct. 2026 However, the defense of our constitutional republic must start somewhere. — Dan Lipinski, Baltimore Sun, 27 Oct. 2026 Locked in a war of attrition with the United States, the Islamic republic has clear objectives.\nL103: — Reuel Marc Gerecht, Washington Post, 23 Oct. 2026 【27†See All Example Sentences for republic】\nL104: ## Word History\nL105: \nL106: Etymology\nL107: \nL108: French république, from Middle French republique, from Latin respublica, from res thing, wealth + publica, feminine of publicus public — more at 【28†real】, 【29†public】\nL109: \nL110: First Known Use\nL111: \nL112: 1596, in the meaning defined at 【30†sense 1a】\nL113: \nL114: Time Traveler\nL115: \nL116: The first known use of republic was in 1596\nL117: \nL118: 【31†See more words from the same year】\nL119: \nL120: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL121: \nL122: 【32†reptiloid】\nL123: \nL124: republic\nL125: \nL126: 【33†republican】\nL127: \nL128: 【34†See all Nearby Words】\nL129: ## Articles Related to republic\nL130: \nL131: 【35†Democracy or Republic: What's the... And which is the United States?】 【36†Forms of Government Quiz How do people take and hold power?】\nL132: \nL133: ## Cite this Entry\nL134: \nL135: Style\nL136: \nL137: [Select]\nL138: \nL139: “Republic.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/republic. Accessed 2 Nov. 2026.\nL140: \nL141: 【37†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】Copy Citation\nL142: ## Kids Definition\nL143: \nL144: republic\nL145: \nL146: noun\nL147: \nL148: re·​pub·​lic 【16†ri-ˈpəb-lik 】\nL149: \nL150: 1\nL151: \nL152: : a government having a chief of state who is not a monarch and who is usually a president\nL153: \nL154: 2\nL155: \nL156: : a government in which supreme power belongs to the citizens through their right to vote\nL157: \nL158: 3\nL159: \nL160: : a political unit having a republican form of government\nL161: \nL162: 4\nL163: \nL164: : a political and territorial unit of the former nations of the U.S.S.R., Czechoslovakia, or Yugoslavia\nL165: \nL166: Etymology\nL167: from French république \"republic,\" derived from Latin respublica \"republic, public matters, commonweal,\" literally \"public things,\" from res \"thing, matter\" and publica, a feminine form of publicus \"relating to the people as a whole, public\" — related to 【38†public】, 【39†real】, 【40†rebus】\nL168: ## Legal Definition\nL169: \nL170: republic\nL171: \nL172: noun\nL173: \nL174: re·​pub·​lic\nL175: \nL176: 1\nL177: \nL178: : a government having a chief of state who is not a monarch and who in modern times is usually a president\nL179: \nL180: also : a political unit (as a nation) having such a form of government\nL181: \nL182: 2\nL183: \nL184: : a government in which supreme power resides in a body of citizens entitled to vote and is exercised by elected officers and representatives responsible to them and governing according to law\nL185: \nL186: also : a political unit (as a nation) having such a form of government\nL187: ## More from Merriam-Webster on republic\nL188: \nL189: Britannica.com: 【41†Encyclopedia article about republic†www.britannica.com】\nL190: \nL191: Love words? Need even more definitions?\nL192: \nL193: Subscribe to America's largest dictionary and get thousands more definitions and advanced search—ad free!\nL194: \nL195: Last Updated: 31 Oct 2026 - Updated example sentences\nL196: \nL197: ## More from Merriam-Webster\nL198: \nL199: #### 【3†Word of the Day】\nL200: \nL201: #### 【3†satiate】\nL202: \nL203: 【3†See Definitions and Examples】 »\nL204: ## Popular in Grammar & Usage\nL205: \nL206: 【4†See More】\nL207: \nL208: 【42†How to Use 'Who' vs. 'Whom'】\nL209: \nL210: 【43†Merriam-Webster’s Great Big List of Words You Love to Hate】\nL211: \nL212: 【44†'Affect' vs. 'Effect'】\nL213: \nL214: 【45†'A' or 'an'? What about before 'h'? An Indefinite Article Guide】\nL215: \nL216: 【46†Why is '-ed' sometimes pronounced at th