ccused him of establishing a dictatorship.\nL83: \nL84: Recent Examples on the Web\nL85: \nL86: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【24†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL87: The first project is about the disappearance of petty criminal El Nani (aka Santiago Correla) in 1980s Spain during the country’s transition out of dictatorship and into democracy. — Jesse Whittock, Deadline, 30 Oct. 2026 Nixon had called Cuba a dictatorship prior to the interview, and has repeated that position since. — Miami Herald, 30 Oct.\nL88: 2026 Von Wernich, a former police chaplain, was sentenced to life in prison in 2007 for his role in seven murders and dozens of kidnappings and torture cases during Argentina’s 1976-1983 military dictatorship. — ABC News, 29 Oct. 2026 As Francisco Franco’s dictatorship waned and tourism surged, Montaner’s vision found success in Mallorca and Benidorm—and eventually crossed the Atlantic. — Alessandra Schade, Vanity Fair, 28 Oct. 2026 【25†See All Example Sentences for dictatorship】\nL89: ## Word History\nL90: \nL91: Etymology\nL92: \nL93: 【18†dictator】 + 【26†-ship】, as translation of Latin dictātūra\nL94: \nL95: First Known Use\nL96: \nL97: 1542, in the meaning defined at 【27†sense 1】\nL98: \nL99: Time Traveler\nL100: \nL101: The first known use of dictatorship was in 1542\nL102: \nL103: 【28†See more words from the same year】\nL104: \nL105: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL106: \nL107: 【29†dictatorian】\nL108: \nL109: dictatorship\nL110: \nL111: 【30†dictatorship of the proletariat】\nL112: \nL113: 【31†See all Nearby Words】\nL114: \nL115: ## Articles Related to dictatorship\nL116: \nL117: 【32†Forms of Government Quiz How do people take and hold power?】\nL118: ## Cite this Entry\nL119: \nL120: Style\nL121: \nL122: [Select]\nL123: \nL124: “Dictatorship.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.